#
# This Containerfile and the image it produces are part of the Wakir
# federation-server substrate. Licensed under the Business Source
# License 1.1; see ./LICENSE-BSL.md.
# Change Date: 2030-05-13 (four years after the first BSL-licensed
# image publication, ``wakir-provisioner:0.1.2``).
# Change License: Apache License 2.0.
#
# Per-wheel licences shipped inside the image (``nats-py`` — Apache-2.0,
# CPython stdlib — PSF-2.0) are unaffected by the image-level BSL
# header; the wheels remain reachable via ``pip show <pkg>`` inside a
# running container, and their licences govern the wheel artefacts as
# the upstream projects ship them.
#
# wakir-provisioner — minimal Python image carrying the wheel set the
# Phase-2 Sprint-9 per-org NATS-KV bucket provisioner needs at runtime.
#
# Why a dedicated image
# ---------------------
# The Sprint-9 Tag-1 Quadlet (``quadlet/wakir-nats-kv-bucket-init.container``)
# was authored against ``docker.io/library/python:3.13-slim`` on the
# assumption that the slim image ships ``nats-py`` pre-installed. It
# does not — slim ships the CPython stdlib only. The Sprint-9 Tag-4
# live-bring-up on the Pilot-VM crashed at unit-start with
# ``ModuleNotFoundError: cryptography`` because the provisioner's
# transitive imports through ``wirelang.federation.marker_stack_kv``
# pulled in ``wirelang.identity`` (the v0.1.0 image carried
# ``cryptography``, ``rfc8785``, ``jsonschema`` to satisfy that chain).
#
# Three options were on the table (Mira-Bug-Bilanz 2026-05-13, Bug 6):
#
#   A) bump the base image to one that ships every wheel the
#      provisioner needs (the v0.1.0 image);
#   B) install wheels at unit start via ``ExecStartPre`` against a
#      tmpfs target — depends on pip reaching PyPI from inside the
#      hardened Quadlet (ReadOnly + DropCapability), and re-introduces
#      a supply-chain network egress on every unit start;
#   C) refactor the provisioner so the transitive Wirelang-imports
#      don't trigger ``cryptography`` (Reza-side, Sprint-9 Tag-4
#      Wirelang-import-disentanglement track, PR #33).
#
# A and C are complementary, not exclusive. A closed the wheel-
# availability gap in v0.1.0; C landed in Reza-PR #33 (PEP-562 lazy
# ``__getattr__`` on ``wirelang.federation``) and shrinks the
# provisioner's runtime wheel need from four wheels to one. This
# v0.1.1 image reflects the post-disentanglement minimum.
#
# Image scope (v0.1.1, post Reza-PR #33)
# --------------------------------------
# The image carries exactly the runtime wheel set:
#
#   * ``nats-py`` — pinned to the same version the orchestrator-side
#     ``requirements-nats.txt`` pins (Sprint-4 Tag-2 First-Time-Live-
#     Smoke version), hash-pinned for supply-chain provenance.
#
# After Reza-PR #33, the previously transitive ``cryptography``,
# ``rfc8785``, ``jsonschema`` imports are gated behind the lazy
# ``wirelang.federation.__getattr__`` and resolve only on identity-
# stack access paths the provisioner does not exercise. The
# bind-mounted host runtime stays importable end-to-end for the
# marker-stack-kv / sequence-number-ledger code paths the provisioner
# does call.
#
# The image deliberately does NOT carry the wakir-runtime tree itself.
# The runtime is bind-mounted into the container from the host at
# ``/opt/wakir-runtime`` (see Quadlet ``Volume=`` lines). Bumping the
# Wirelang/runtime tree does not require an image rebuild; bumping
# the ``nats-py`` pin above does.
#
# BSL-1.1 + image provenance
# --------------------------
# ``nats-py`` ships under Apache-2.0. The image as a whole is published
# under the Business Source License 1.1 (AR-Decision 2026-05-13,
# consistent with the WAT-Pipeline-Server Phase-1a BSL pattern per
# ADR-0034). The per-wheel licence text ships in the wheel itself and
# is reachable via ``pip show nats-py`` inside a running container;
# the transitive wheel licences (Apache-2.0, BSD) are unaffected by
# the BSL header on the image as a whole. The BSL Additional Use
# Grant permits self-hosting for own-org operations; commercial
# multi-tenant hosting requires a separate Wakir-Cloud licence. The
# image automatically converts to Apache-2.0 four years after the
# first BSL-licensed image publication (Change Date: 2030-05-13).
#
# Build path: ``.github/workflows/build-wakir-provisioner.yml``
# (Operator-Hand ``workflow_dispatch`` only; pushes to
# ``ghcr.io/wakir-labs/wakir-provisioner:<tag>``).
#
# Pin path: ``infra/spire/federation/IMAGE_PINS.md`` §1 table carries
# the ``ghcr.io/wakir-labs/wakir-provisioner`` row; the Quadlet
# references the digest-pinned form
# ``ghcr.io/wakir-labs/wakir-provisioner:<tag>@sha256:<digest>``.

FROM docker.io/library/python:3.13-slim@sha256:DIGEST_PENDING_TOMAS_REVIEW

# OCI metadata. The build workflow stamps build-time labels
# (``org.opencontainers.image.revision`` etc.) at push time.
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="wakir-provisioner"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.description="Runtime image for the Wakir per-org NATS-KV bucket provisioner (Sprint-9 Tag-4, v0.1.2 BSL-1.1 relicense: nats-py only; AR-Decision 2026-05-13)."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.version="0.1.2"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.licenses="BUSL-1.1"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.source="https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.url="https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.documentation="https://github.com/wakir-labs/wakir-runtime/blob/main/infra/spire/federation/provisioner/README.md"

# Hash-pinned requirements file. The wheel digests are sourced from
# the PyPI JSON API on the build host (Operator-Hand) and recorded in
# the in-repo file ``provisioner/requirements.txt``. Hash mismatch
# halts the build by design (``--require-hashes``).
COPY requirements.txt /tmp/requirements.txt

# Install with hash verification, no cache, no compile bytecode. The
# build host is the only place that needs PyPI network egress; the
# runtime image carries everything pre-installed.
RUN pip install \
        --no-cache-dir \
        --no-compile \
        --require-hashes \
        --requirement /tmp/requirements.txt \
    && rm -f /tmp/requirements.txt

# Non-root runtime user, matching the Quadlet ``User=1000`` /
# ``Group=1000``. We do not chown ``/opt/wakir`` because the runtime
# bind-mount supplies its own ownership semantics at unit start.
RUN groupadd --gid 1000 wakir \
    && useradd --uid 1000 --gid 1000 --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin wakir

USER 1000:1000
WORKDIR /opt/wakir-runtime

# No ENTRYPOINT, no CMD.
#
# Sprint-9 Tag-6 (Bug 6 from Live-Bring-up-2-Bilanz 2026-05-14):
# the v0.1.2 image previously baked ``ENTRYPOINT ["python3"]`` plus
# a default ``CMD ["--version"]``. The bucket-init Quadlet supplies
# its own ``Exec=python3 /opt/wakir/bin/nats-kv-bucket-provision
# ...`` line; the result is the entrypoint+exec concatenation
# ``python3 python3 /opt/wakir/bin/...`` where the second
# ``python3`` is interpreted as a script path relative to
# ``WORKDIR=/opt/wakir-runtime`` and crashes the container at unit
# start with ``python3: can't open file '/opt/wakir-runtime/python3'``.
#
# Fix: the Quadlet stays the canonical caller and supplies the
# Python interpreter explicitly (Bug-8-Pfad-B per Kai's quadlet edit
# in the matching Tag-6 PR). To keep the image robust against any
# OTHER caller that also supplies its own interpreter, we drop the
# ENTRYPOINT entirely and leave CMD empty: the image is strictly
# caller-driven (defence-in-depth).
#
# Operator-Hand callers that want a one-shot ``python3 --version``
# smoke can still use ``podman run --rm <image> python3 --version``;
# they explicitly supply the interpreter. The provisioner-domain
# convention is documented in
# ``infra/spire/federation/provisioner/README.md``.
